  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who the Grand Floridian Villas are best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Families who want a monorail to Magic Kingdom and a kitchen, without moving to a different part of property.</strong> The villas share the hotel's monorail station, boat dock and restaurants.</li>
      <li><strong>Groups who need a fifth bed in a studio.</strong> Both the Deluxe Studio (pull-downs) and the Resort Studio (two queens and a day bed) are listed as sleeping five.</li>
      <li><strong>People who already like the Grand Floridian and want more space than a hotel room.</strong> This is not a quieter, cheaper resort across the lagoon; it is the same campus at villa prices.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The nightly rate is the deciding factor.</strong> In 2026 a Deluxe Studio – Resort View ran about $832 to $1,370 a night at full price, tax included, and Grand Villas ran much higher.</li>
      <li><strong>You want a standalone villa resort with its own dining.</strong> There is no separate villas restaurant; you walk to the hotel.</li>
      <li><strong>You were picturing a standard hotel room.</strong> The photos on this page are villa interiors, not the hotel rooms covered in the Grand Floridian guide.</li>
    </ul>
